<v Man 4>I saw him. And the guy, he stopped, and he stopped, talking to somebody. I think he asked for a cigarette.
<v Man 4>我看见他了。那个人，他停下来了，他停下来和某人交谈。我想他要了支烟。

00:46:26.400 --> 00:46:35.970
<v Dana Costello>And the two women stood back a little bit. And he came up, and he was very humble, and he asked for some money for food. He didn't seem threatening.
<v Dana Costello>那两个女人稍微退后了一点。他走过来，表现得很谦卑，他要了一些食物钱。他看起来不像是有威胁。

00:46:35.970 --> 00:46:51.330
<v Man 4>But I saw the girl eye to eye, and she looked familiar. Even that, her pictures, were in the door, you know? But it did not click. It did not click.
<v Man 4>但我跟那个女孩对视过，她看起来很眼熟。即使是那样，她的照片都在门上，你知道吗？但就是没对上号。没对上号。

00:46:51.330 --> 00:47:04.840
<v Scott Carrier>It wasn't like they just showed up out of nowhere. Mitchell and Barzee had been homeless, on the street, wearing their robes for a couple years before they took Elizabeth. My friend, Trent Harris, thought he knew the guy all too well.
<v Scott Carrier>这不像他们凭空出现一样。Mitchell和Barzee在绑架Elizabeth之前，已经在街上流浪、穿着长袍几年了。我的朋友Trent Harris觉得他太了解那个家伙了。

00:47:04.840 --> 00:47:19.950
<v Trent Harris>Well, he was kind of a fixture on the streets of Salt Lake City. He was always asking for money in front of the ZCMI Shopping Mall. I remember he'd sit out there with this just pathetic look on his face. Oh, he was annoying. He was really annoying.
<v Trent Harris>嗯，他是盐湖城市中心街头的一道风景线。他总是坐在ZCMI购物中心前要钱。我记得他坐在那儿，脸上带着那种可怜巴巴的表情。噢，他很烦人。他真的太烦人了。

00:47:19.950 --> 00:47:25.220
<v Milo>I'd seen them before, and I thought they were just crazy extremist fundamentalists who'd walk around.
<v Milo>我以前见过他们，我以为他们只是到处走动的疯狂极端原教旨主义者。

00:47:25.220 --> 00:47:47.290
<v Scott Carrier>That's my son, Milo. He's the same age as Elizabeth and went to grade school with her. We have a picture of Milo and Elizabeth at a birthday party when they were four. He's dressed as a cowboy. She's a princess. My wife, Hilary, gave her dance lessons. In September, Milo saw Elizabeth with Mitchell and Barzee when he was at a gas station washing the car.
<v Scott Carrier>那是我儿子，Milo。他和Elizabeth同岁，和她一起上小学。我们有一张Milo和Elizabeth四岁时在生日派对上的照片。他扮成牛仔。她是公主。我妻子Hilary教她跳舞。九月份，Milo在加油站洗车时看到了Elizabeth和Mitchell还有Barzee。

00:47:47.290 --> 00:47:54.050
<v Milo>They walked by me. I was within 10 feet of them. I'm pretty sure she could've recognized me if she'd just seen me. I bet she did.
<v Milo>他们从我身边走过。我就在他们10英尺范围内。我很确定如果她见过我的话，她能认出我。我打赌她一定认出我了。

00:47:54.050 --> 00:47:59.000
<v Scott Carrier>But she didn't say anything, and Milo didn't recognize her.
<v Scott Carrier>但她什么也没说，Milo也没认出她。

00:47:59.000 --> 00:48:26.290
<v Milo>I didn't think anything of it, because that's ridiculous. Like, she's walking around in the middle of daylight. It wouldn't be her. It didn't even cross my mind that it would be her. Who would do that? Who would actually take the person that everybody is looking for, the most sought after person in the whole state pretty much? That's risking your life. If somebody found him and recognized her, they would've killed him.
<v Milo>我没多想，因为那太荒谬了。我是说，她在大白天走来走去。不可能是她。我脑子里根本没闪过那是她的念头。谁会这么做？谁会真的带走全州都在寻找的人，基本上是全州最受关注的人？那是在冒生命危险。如果有人发现并认出她，他们会杀了他。

00:48:26.290 --> 00:48:53.040
<v Scott   Carrier>Part of the reason the disguise worked is because, whether we saw him as a pest, or a harmless eccentric, or a crazy fundamentalist, we saw him as a loser. Someone to be pitied or scorned, written off, ignored, forgotten. And we don't really look at people like that. We look away.
<v Scott   Carrier>伪装奏效的部分原因是，无论我们将他视为害虫、无害的怪人，还是疯狂的原教旨主义者，我们都把他看作一个失败者。一个值得怜悯或鄙视、被写掉、被忽视、被遗忘的人。我们并没有真正注视那样的人。我们移开视线。

00:48:53.040 --> 00:49:12.480
<v Scott   Carrier>There's another reason we didn't recognize Elizabeth, and it has to do with a thing that's specific to this culture and this place right here. In any other state, if you saw Mitchell and Barzee walking along with a young girl in tow, you'd think it was their daughter. But when we saw it here, we thought she was his new polygamist wife.
<v Scott   Carrier>我们没认出Elizabeth还有另一个原因，这与此地特有的文化有关。在任何其他州，如果你看到Mitchell和Barzee带着一个年轻女孩，你会认为那是他们的女儿。但当我们在这里看到它时，我们认为她是他的新多妻制妻子。

00:49:12.480 --> 00:49:25.440
<v Trent  Harris>And I remember saying to myself, you know, I'm used to seeing Emmanuel David with one woman, but now he had two, and the other one was much younger. And I remember thinking, how did he manage to pull that off?
<v Trent  Harris>我记得对自己说，你知道，我习惯看到Emmanuel David带一个女人，但现在他有两个，另一个要年轻得多。我记得我在想，他是怎么搞定那个的？

00:49:25.440 --> 00:49:31.390
<v Dana  Costello>And when he came up to us the day at Wendy's, I felt bad for those two women..
<v Dana  Costello>当他在温迪那天走到我们面前时，我为那两个女人感到难过。

00:49:31.390 --> 00:49:33.240
<v Scott Carrier>Here's my friend, Dana Costello.
<v Scott Carrier>这是我的朋友，Dana Costello。

00:49:33.240 --> 00:49:45.500
<v Dana Costello>And I especially noticed that the other one was young. But I didn't think that's their daughter. I assumed immediately, just in my head, that she was his new wife. It just came to my head. Oh, look, he got another wife.
<v Dana Costello>我特别注意到另一个很年轻。但我没想那是他们的女儿。我立刻就在脑子里假设她是他的新妻子。这直接就进到我脑子里了。噢，看，他又找了个老婆。

00:49:45.500 --> 00:49:49.980
<v Scott Carrier>You didn't anything bad about it when you saw that he had two wives.
<v Scott Carrier>你看到他有两个老婆时，没觉得有什么不对劲。

00:49:49.980 --> 00:49:51.790
<v Dana Costello>I didn't. I really didn't.
<v Dana Costello>我没觉得。我真的没觉得。

00:49:51.790 --> 00:50:20.460
<v Scott   Carrier>Officially, the Mormons stopped believing in polygamy in 1890, but it still goes on here and there. And many people in Salt Lake City have polygamist ancestors. And when we run into it, there's a sense of shame and embarrassment mixed in with American ideals of freedom of religion. And the end result is we just ignore it and let it be. We don't want to look at it.
<v Scott   Carrier>官方上，摩门教徒在1890年停止信仰多妻制，但这在这里时有发生。盐湖城的许多人都有多妻制的祖先。当我们遇到它时，会有一种羞耻感和尴尬感，夹杂着美国的宗教自由理想。最终结果就是我们只是忽视它，顺其自然。我们不想看它。

00:50:20.460 --> 00:50:54.090
<v Scott   Carrier>What's especially annoying about this is the possibility that Mitchell was smart enough to figure it out and use this weakness against us. The most amazing encounter with Elizabeth happened in September, not long before the threesome left town to spend the winter in southern California. There was a party in a big house full of bohemians. Mitchell, Barzee, and Elizabeth were walking by, maybe on their way back to their camp in the foothills, and they stopped in.
<v Scott   Carrier>特别让人恼火的是，Mitchell可能足够聪明，能弄明白这一点并利用这个弱点对付我们。最令人震惊的与Elizabeth的相遇发生在九月份，就在这三人离开城镇去南加州过冬之前不久。有一个派对在充满波西米亚风格的大房子里举行。Mitchell、Barzee和Elizabeth路过，也许是在回山脚下营地的路上，他们停下来了。

00:50:54.090 --> 00:50:56.550
<v Scott   Carrier>Amber Meriwether and Russell Ferrell were there that night.
<v Scott   Carrier>Amber Meriwether和Russell Ferrell那天晚上在那儿。

00:50:56.550 --> 00:51:07.360
<v Amber Meriwether>There's always people in costumes at the parties there. So we just thought that they were just people in costume, just being silly.
<v Amber Meriwether>那里的派对上总有人穿奇装异服。所以我们只是以为他们是穿戏服的人，只是在闹着玩。

00:51:07.360 --> 00:51:19.550
<v Russell Ferrell>He drew a lot of attention to himself, because he had a lot of preaching going on. He was an obvious preacher, but it seemed dichotomous that he was meanwhile bumming beers.
<v Russell Ferrell>他吸引了很多注意力，因为他一直在布道。他是个明显的传教士，但与此同时他在蹭啤酒，这看起来很矛盾。

00:51:19.550 --> 00:51:26.860
<v Amber Meriwether>It looked like he had never seen food or drink in his entire life. The man's was obviously starving and a complete obnoxious drunk.
<v Amber Meriwether>看起来他这辈子从没见过食物或饮料一样。那个人显然饿坏了，而且是个令人讨厌的醉鬼。

00:51:26.860 --> 00:51:35.900
<v Russell Ferrell>Finally, he got a hold of some local-made absinthe, which is the tincture of wormwood made famous by the impressionists back in the turn of the century.
<v Russell Ferrell>最后，他搞到了一些当地酿造的苦艾酒，就是世纪之交印象派画家们出名的苦艾草酊剂。

00:51:35.900 --> 00:51:40.980
<v Amber Meriwether>He was just drinking it like crazy, and he was getting super sloshed. And he was trying to get the two girls to drink it.
<v Amber Meriwether>他就在疯狂地喝，喝得酩酊大醉。他还试图让那两个女孩喝。

00:51:40.980 --> 00:52:03.020
<v Scott Carrier>Somebody at the party took a picture of Elizabeth standing there in the kitchen. You may have seen it, a veil across her face, her eyes so clearly now the same eyes in the posters hanging up all over town. The people at the party were so close, but still they couldn't see her. It was just too absurd. Amber even went up and talked to her.
<v Scott Carrier>派对上的某个人拍了一张Elizabeth站在厨房的照片。你可能见过，面纱遮住她的脸，她眼神现在很清楚，就是挂在城里海报上的那双眼睛。派对上的人离得那么近，却依然看不见她。这太荒谬了。Amber甚至走过去和她说话。

00:52:03.020 --> 00:52:22.430
<v Amber Meriwether>I walked into the kitchen, and I remember looking at her a couple of times and thinking that she looked familiar. I had been seeing her pictures everywhere, but I didn't connect the two. I just thought, I mean, come on, you're not going to think Elizabeth Smart's going to be drunk at a party. Or I guess she wasn't drunk. I don't think she drank anything, actually.
<v Amber Meriwether>我走进厨房，我记得看着她好几次，心想她看起来很眼熟。我到处都在看她的照片，但我没把两者联系起来。我只是想，我是说，得了吧，你不会认为Elizabeth Smart会醉倒在一个派对上。或者我想她没醉。其实我觉得她什么都没喝。

00:52:22.430 --> 00:52:36.340
<v Amber Meriwether>But I walked up to her and I just asked her, where do I know her from? That she looks really familiar. And she didn't really say anything back. And the man, Emmanuel, came up, he said, no, you don't know her.
<v Amber Meriwether>但我走过去问她，我是从哪儿认识她的？她看起来真的很眼熟。她没说什么。那个男人，Emmanuel，走过来说，不，你不认识她。

00:52:36.340 --> 00:52:56.440
<v Russell Ferrell>Someone said, well, was this guy using some sort of black magic over the whole scenario? Putting a veil, so to speak, over everyone's perception? His approach to her and the other woman was very-- he was quite the patriarch.
<v Russell Ferrell>有人说，好吧，这人是对整个场景用了什么黑魔法吗？给每个人的认知都蒙上了一层面纱？他对她和另一个女人的那种方式很——他表现得很有大家长风范。

00:52:56.440 --> 00:53:29.410
<v Amber  Meriwether>I think he was so believing of his righteousness or his potency of his prophecy, and he would do these little things to prove to himself, more and more so, that he was being protected by God because it was right what he was doing, that every time he took Elizabeth out and that she wasn't noticed, it was true that Elizabeth was his. And he really believed that he could be protected in this way, that he made it happen in this weird way. It did happen.
<v Amber  Meriwether>我认为他太相信自己的正义感或预言的能力了，他会做这些小动作来向自己证明，越多越好，因为他所做的一切是正确的，所以他受到了上帝的保护，每当他把Elizabeth带出去而没人注意到时，Elizabeth是他的就是真的。他真的相信他能以这种方式受到保护，他以这种奇怪的方式让它发生了。它确实发生了。

00:53:29.410 --> 00:53:37.030
<v Amber  Meriwether>He had literally put a spell over Salt Lake City, that no one would see him. It was magical. I think he believed that.
<v Amber  Meriwether>他真的给盐湖城施了咒，没人会看见他。那是魔法。我认为他是这么相信的。

00:53:37.030 --> 00:54:09.500
<v Scott  Carrier>I think it's more likely we didn't see Elizabeth because we thought the guy who took her would be a monster, a bogeyman, and we expected him to appear in that form. Mitchell looked like the opposite of a bogeyman. A bogeyman stands in the shadows and jumps out at you. Mitchell stood right in front of us and sort of became invisible.
<v Scott  Carrier>我觉得更有可能是我们没看见Elizabeth，因为我们认为带走她的人会是个怪物，是个妖魔，我们期待他以那种形式出现。Mitchell看起来和妖魔恰恰相反。妖魔站在阴影里跳出来吓你。Mitchell就站在我们面前，某种程度上变成了隐形。

00:54:09.500 --> 00:54:35.860
<v Scott  Carrier>When Elizabeth was discovered nine months after her abduction, we all realized our mistake. And it was like a combination of being really happy she was alive mixed with feelings of being duped in a rather serious and sinister way. We realized we were partly to blame, that there was something within us that made us deny the obvious. And that hurt. My son, Milo, thought about it for months afterwards. He's 17 now.
<v Scott  Carrier>Elizabeth在绑架九个月后被发现时，我们都意识到我们错了。那感觉就像是既非常高兴她还活着，又夹杂着一种被以相当严重和险恶的方式愚弄的感受。我们意识到我们要负部分责任，我们内心有某种东西让我们否认了显而易见的事情。那很痛。我儿子，Milo，在那之后的几个月里一直在想这个。他现在17岁了。

00:54:35.860 --> 00:54:39.180
<v Scott Carrier>Did you feel bad when you realized that you'd missed it?
<v Scott Carrier>当你意识到你错过了它时，你觉得难过吗？

00:54:39.180 --> 00:54:40.490
<v Milo>Yeah.
<v Milo>是的。

00:54:40.490 --> 00:54:42.750
<v Scott Carrier>Tell me what you felt like when you heard it.
<v Scott Carrier>告诉我当你听到它时你是什么感觉。

00:54:42.750 --> 00:55:25.030
<v Milo>Well, I mean, when I heard that she was walking around with that guy, it was like, holy crap, I've been right there. Like, I've walked by those guys, and just passed it off as nothing. It sounds bad, but the way that he kept her was he convinced her to stay with him. He took her up into the mountains and-- he's not a good guy. He's a terrible person. He took her up into the mountains and somehow brainwashed her, convinced her that she was meant to be with him and he was the son of God. He thinks he's Jesus.
<v Milo>嗯，我是说，当我听说她和那个人走在一起时，那感觉就像，天哪，我就在那儿。就像，我走过那些人身边，却把它当成什么都没有。这听起来很糟糕，但他困住她的方式是他说服她留在他身边。他带她上山，然后——他不是个好人。他是个糟糕的人。他带她上山，不知怎么洗了她的脑，让她相信她注定要和他在一起，他是上帝之子。他以为他是耶稣。

00:55:25.030 --> 00:55:41.510
<v Milo>If I could have been there-- Like, if I had known then, if I had just been able to think about it-- I don't know, it made me pretty angry. Like, maybe if I could just go back to then, that day. Thinking about that more than anything.
<v Milo>如果我能在那儿——就像，如果我当时就知道，如果我当时能想到——我不知道，这让我很生气。就像，也许如果我能回到那时，那一天。想这个比想什么都多。

00:55:41.510 --> 00:56:30.070
<v Scott   Carrier>I saw Elizabeth a couple weeks ago, downtown between the shopping malls. She was on one side of the street and I was on the other. At first, I thought she was a woman in her 20s, maybe a legal secretary, because that's how she was dressed. But then, when the light changed and we walked by each other, I thought, who is that? She looks like a young Mariel Hemingway, really distinctive and beautiful eyes. And then I realized it was Elizabeth Smart, right as she went by me. It was like seeing a rock star or a mythic heroine. She'd journeyed to a dark world, and it seemed, looking at her, that she'd come back with her innocence intact. And this made me feel hopeful, like maybe things are going to turn out OK.
<v Scott   Carrier>几周前我在市中心商场之间见到了Elizabeth。她在街的一边，我在另一边。起初，我以为她是个20多岁的女人，也许是个法律秘书，因为她是那样打扮的。但后来，当绿灯亮起，我们走过彼此身边时，我想，那是谁？她看起来像年轻时的Mariel Hemingway，非常独特且美丽的眼睛。然后我意识到是Elizabeth Smart，就在她从我身边经过时。那感觉就像看到一个摇滚明星或神话英雄。她去过一个黑暗的世界，看着她，感觉她似乎带回了她纯真的本质。这让我感到充满希望，也许一切都会好起来的。

00:56:30.070 --> 00:56:46.220
<v Ira       Glass>Scott Carrier in Salt Lake City. His story received support from hearingvoices.com, which gets some of its support from The Corporation for Public Broadcasting.
<v Ira       Glass>Scott Carrier在盐湖城。他的故事得到了hearingvoices.com的支持，该网站得到公共广播公司的部分支持。

